<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bonn Republic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was the common English name for the Federal Republic of Germany between its foundation in 1949 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-reunification">German reunification</a> in 1990. Formed from the American, British and French occupation zones with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> as its capital, it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> and a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>. Its rapid post-war recovery became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a>, or economic miracle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-germany.jpg" alt="West Germany" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp, Madden, and other users · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>West Germany</b> was established as a federal parliamentary republic with a social market economy under the Grundgesetz, the Basic Law of 1949.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Second World War, American aid, tourism, shipping and rapid urbanisation produced high growth in the 1950s and 1960s, while hundreds of thousands emigrated to <b>West Germany</b> and Australia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…for domestic production expanded industrial output, and companies evaded the embargo through subsidiaries and a clandestine trade network: <b>West Germany</b> remained the largest Western European trading partner until 1973, Japan the chief market outside Africa, Iran supplied oil, Portugal re-exported goods through Mozambique with false certificates of origin,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…million foreign tourists visited Dalmatia in 1965 and 4.75 million in 1970, bringing $275 million, a tenth of federal revenue, by 1969; emigration was legalised in 1963, and by the early 1970s 1.1 million workers, a fifth of the labour force, worked abroad, two thirds in <b>West Germany</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…into self-governing units can be both large and diverse without a central government turning tyrannical, and that dividing power makes it harder for a would-be dictator to seize it all; for this reason the Western Allies required the constitution of <b>West Germany</b>, adopted in 1949, to be federal.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 parliamentary democracy was rebuilt under Allied occupation in Italy, in <b>West Germany</b>, whose Basic Law of 1949 introduced the constructive vote of no confidence, and in Japan, whose constitution of 1947 made the cabinet answerable to the Diet.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
